1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,438
Open your camera's menu

2
00:00:02,238 --> 00:00:05,631
and you'll see twenty-three point nine eight.

3
00:00:06,161 --> 00:00:08,847
The story starts a hundred years ago.

4
00:00:09,834 --> 00:00:12,114
August, nineteen twenty-six.

5
00:00:11,914 --> 00:00:12,815
New York.

6
00:00:13,222 --> 00:00:14,741
At the Warners' Theatre

7
00:00:14,541 --> 00:00:16,663
the film Don Juan opens.

8
00:00:18,152 --> 00:00:19,920
As the screen lights up

9
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:21,953
an orchestra begins to play.

10
00:00:21,753 --> 00:00:24,711
But there isn't a single musician in the pit.

11
00:00:25,661 --> 00:00:28,778
The music is coming from the projection booth

12
00:00:28,578 --> 00:00:32,002
from a giant record spinning beside the projector

13
00:00:31,802 --> 00:00:33,613
driven by the same motor.

14
00:00:35,012 --> 00:00:37,736
Warner Bros., called it Vitaphone.

15
00:00:39,361 --> 00:00:40,915
But if the needle skipped

16
00:00:40,715 --> 00:00:42,861
the sound ran ahead of the picture

17
00:00:42,661 --> 00:00:44,185
or fell behind.

18
00:00:45,212 --> 00:00:47,240
Then the projectionist had to put

19
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:49,004
the needle back in place by hand.

20
00:00:50,588 --> 00:00:54,251
And every theater ran its projectors at a different speed.

21
00:00:55,237 --> 00:00:57,978
Western Electric built the Vitaphone equipment.

22
00:00:59,151 --> 00:01:00,699
One of its engineers

23
00:01:00,499 --> 00:01:01,809
Stanley Watkins

24
00:01:01,609 --> 00:01:03,871
asked a projectionist how fast

25
00:01:03,671 --> 00:01:06,171
theaters actually ran their film.

26
00:01:09,008 --> 00:01:11,524
The projectionist said big first-run

27
00:01:11,324 --> 00:01:13,782
houses ran eighty to ninety feet a minute

28
00:01:13,582 --> 00:01:16,082
and small theaters over a hundred.

29
00:01:17,968 --> 00:01:20,532
Watkins picked one number in between.

30
00:01:20,332 --> 00:01:22,638
Ninety feet a minute.

31
00:01:24,275 --> 00:01:26,442
In a nineteen seventy interview

32
00:01:26,242 --> 00:01:27,756
Watkins put it this way:

33
00:01:27,556 --> 00:01:29,648
"That had to be a good guess."

34
00:01:31,191 --> 00:01:33,775
One foot of thirty-five millimeter

35
00:01:33,575 --> 00:01:35,485
film holds sixteen frames.

36
00:01:35,285 --> 00:01:39,100
Ninety feet a minute is twenty-four frames a second.

37
00:01:42,829 --> 00:01:44,427
The next October

38
00:01:44,227 --> 00:01:45,926
in The Jazz Singer

39
00:01:45,726 --> 00:01:49,322
audiences heard an actor sing and speak.

40
00:01:50,022 --> 00:01:53,555
Fox Movietone, which printed sound onto the film itself

41
00:01:53,355 --> 00:01:55,257
chose the same twenty-four.

42
00:01:55,911 --> 00:02:00,388
From then on, twenty-four frames a second was the standard in theaters.

43
00:02:01,088 --> 00:02:02,972
By nineteen fifty-three

44
00:02:02,772 --> 00:02:06,821
Americans had already bought many black-and-white TVs.

45
00:02:07,549 --> 00:02:11,924
Black-and-white broadcasts sent thirty pictures a second.

46
00:02:11,724 --> 00:02:15,873
What broadcasters prepared next was color.

47
00:02:16,573 --> 00:02:18,126
In nineteen fifty

48
00:02:17,926 --> 00:02:21,622
the FCC approved CBS's color system.

49
00:02:21,422 --> 00:02:24,557
But the sets people already owned couldn't show it.

50
00:02:28,775 --> 00:02:30,985
In March nineteen fifty-three

51
00:02:30,785 --> 00:02:32,715
CBS gave it up.

52
00:02:35,005 --> 00:02:37,353
On December seventeenth that year

53
00:02:37,153 --> 00:02:41,900
the FCC approved a new color system

54
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:43,677
that old sets could still show:

55
00:02:43,477 --> 00:02:44,089
NTSC.

56
00:02:45,277 --> 00:02:48,451
NTSC kept the black-and-white signal as it was

57
00:02:48,251 --> 00:02:51,197
and added the color information inside it.

58
00:02:51,597 --> 00:02:53,779
But the added color signal was too

59
00:02:53,579 --> 00:02:56,115
close in frequency to the sound signal.

60
00:02:57,395 --> 00:03:00,008
The difference between the two showed up as a

61
00:02:59,808 --> 00:03:02,747
fine pattern of dots on black-and-white screens.

62
00:03:02,947 --> 00:03:05,273
Moving the sound would fix it

63
00:03:05,073 --> 00:03:08,593
but every set already sold would lose its sound.

64
00:03:08,393 --> 00:03:10,372
The FCC wouldn't allow it.

65
00:03:14,688 --> 00:03:17,984
The NTSC committee's engineers went the other way.

66
00:03:17,784 --> 00:03:20,025
They slowed the picture down

67
00:03:19,825 --> 00:03:21,079
just slightly.

68
00:03:22,602 --> 00:03:26,053
Old sets followed the timing of the broadcast signal

69
00:03:25,853 --> 00:03:29,452
so a slowdown of a tenth of a percent didn't bother them.

70
00:03:31,028 --> 00:03:34,840
The picture slowed to a thousand over a thousand and one.

71
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:38,956
Thirty frames became twenty-nine point nine seven.

72
00:03:39,945 --> 00:03:41,841
So what happened to the movies?

73
00:03:43,236 --> 00:03:45,990
To broadcast twenty-four frame films

74
00:03:45,790 --> 00:03:48,643
on twenty-nine point nine seven TV

75
00:03:48,443 --> 00:03:51,259
the films had to slow down by the same amount.

76
00:03:53,697 --> 00:03:56,878
Twenty-four, slowed by a tenth of a percent:

77
00:03:56,678 --> 00:03:59,756
twenty-three point nine seven six.

78
00:04:00,495 --> 00:04:02,408
Back to twenty twenty-six.

79
00:04:02,208 --> 00:04:03,995
Open the camera menu

80
00:04:03,795 --> 00:04:06,800
and twenty-three point nine eight is still there.

81
00:04:07,876 --> 00:04:11,326
Twenty-three point nine eight is the twenty-four

82
00:04:11,126 --> 00:04:13,835
Watkins chose in nineteen twenty-six

83
00:04:13,635 --> 00:04:16,356
multiplied by the slowdown of nineteen

84
00:04:16,156 --> 00:04:19,607
fifty-three that protected the TVs already sold.

85
00:04:23,107 --> 00:04:25,267
Next time:, Kodak.

86
00:04:25,067 --> 00:04:27,151
In nineteen seventy-five

87
00:04:26,951 --> 00:04:30,704
Kodak built a digital camera

88
00:04:30,504 --> 00:04:31,097
and never sold it.

89
00:04:30,897 --> 00:04:31,501
Why?
